  "protocol_version": "v3",
  "resisted": "Nothing structural. Hidden nodes 2627 and 9555 (the always-on service-call encoding) were split so the near-dead state:2/state:44 unit 252 could be its own leaf; 161 and 3861 are identically 0 on train (dead). The gate under-predicts churn for frequent callers with low day usage (CSC>=4 and day minutes <160: 113 rows, model 0.66 vs empirical 0.88) and over-predicts it for low-usage customers with few calls (CSC<4, day minutes <120: 476 rows, 0.08 vs 0.05). closed_form_max_abs_err 0.031 is 3-significant-figure rounding on state one-hots (standardised values up to 8); the numeric function is exact.",
  "tool_bugs": "split_node cannot re-split a consolidated copy ('Cannot re-split -71_bde: expected 3 outputs, got 5'), so the hidden splits (2627, 9555) had to come before the input splits (undo_operation from seq 71, then redo). The Bash sandbox refused shell loops/variables around uv, so tool calls were driven from a scratch Python script that runs experiments/species_leads/xn.py per call (all calls logged).",
  "summary": "logit P(churn) = -1.05 + a sparse linear backbone over 40 inputs (day minutes +2.04 per SD, voicemail plan -0.90, international plan +0.76, international charge +0.71, voicemail messages +0.62, ...) + a service-call x low-day-usage gate + a 4+-calls hinge + an evening-minutes hinge + exactly linear voicemail and state-offset units, all additive in the logit. The gate is a low-day-minutes hinge whose kink moves up with each customer-service call (median 147 day minutes at 0 calls, 220 at 4, 276 at 6), so with the direct day-minutes edge the logit is V-shaped in day minutes and frequent callers with low usage churn. Service calls act only through hinges. Train AUC 0.901, accuracy 0.901.",
